 "footnotes": [
  {
   "id": "c-term-symbols",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book writes the term symbols ${}^3S_{1/2}$, ${}^3P_{1/2}$ and ${}^3P_{3/2}$ in this sentence. Sodium has one valence electron, so $S = 1/2$ and $2S + 1 = 2$: the levels are the doublets ${}^2S_{1/2}$, ${}^2P_{1/2}$ and ${}^2P_{3/2}$."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-h-shifts",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book evaluates $F^2$, $I^2$ and $S^2$ as the squares of the quantum numbers. With the eigenvalues $F(F+1)$, $I(I+1)$ and $S(S+1)$, $\\kHHFS = (\\kAHFS/2)[F(F+1) - 3/2]$, and the shifts are $(\\kAHFS/4, -3\\kAHFS/4)$; the book prints $(\\kAHFS/4, -\\kAHFS/4)$."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-h-splitting",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "With the eigenvalues of $F^2$, $I^2$ and $S^2$, the two levels are split by $\\kAHFS/4 - (-3\\kAHFS/4) = \\kAHFS$; the book prints $\\kAHFS/2$."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-na-splitting",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "With $I = 3/2$ and the eigenvalues $F(F+1)$, $I(I+1)$ and $S(S+1)$, the shifts are $3\\kAHFS/4$ for $F = 2$ and $-5\\kAHFS/4$ for $F = 1$, so the two levels are separated by $2\\kAHFS$; the book prints $3\\kAHFS/2$, from the squares of the quantum numbers."
  }
 ],
